window.docContents[87].resolve({"/Hoare/#Hoare-Logic--Hoare___-Hoare-Logic___-Part-I--Hoare-Triples___-Formally":{"contents":"We formalize valid Hoare triples in Lean as follows:\n\ndef ValidHoareTriple\n    (P : Assertion) (c : Com) (Q : Assertion) : Prop :=\n  ∀ st st',\n    (st =[ c ]=> st') →\n    P st →\n    Q st'\n\n\n\n\nNotation for Hoare triples.  The command between the two assertions is\nparsed with the same grammar as the imp { … } notation, so a command\nthat is a Lean variable (rather than concrete syntax) is spliced in with\n~c, just as in the st =[ c ]=> st' notation.\n\nopen scoped Assertion\n\nnamespace ValidHoareTriple\n\n/-- Hoare triple: `{{ P }} c {{ Q }}` -/\nscoped syntax:lead \"{{\" term \"}} \" imp_com:lead \" {{\" term \"}}\" : term\n\nmacro_rules\n  | `({{ $P }} $c:imp_com {{ $Q }}) =>\n      `(ValidHoareTriple ({{ $P }}) (imp { $c }) ({{ $Q }}))\n\nend ValidHoareTriple\n\nsection\nopen scoped ValidHoareTriple\n\n\n\n\n\n\nProve that if Q holds in every state, then any triple with Q\nas its postcondition is valid.theorem hoare_post_true (P Q : Assertion) (c : Com) (h : ∀ st, Q st) :\n    {{ P }} ~c {{ Q }} := by\n  sorry\n\n\nProve that if P holds in no state, then any triple with P as\nits precondition is valid.theorem hoare_pre_false (P Q : Assertion) (c : Com) (h : ∀ st, ¬ (P st)) :\n    {{ P }} ~c {{ Q }} := by\n  sorry\n\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic\u0009Hoare: Hoare Logic, Part I","header":"5.3. Hoare Triples, Formally","id":"/Hoare/#Hoare-Logic--Hoare___-Hoare-Logic___-Part-I--Hoare-Triples___-Formally"}});